CAT = "sgsnbEmergency"; SEG = "EMG"
Q = [
('A patient collapses and does not respond. After checking safety, what does the nurse do first?',
 'Check response and call for help', ['Give two rescue breaths straight away', 'Start a full set of blood pressure checks', 'Move the patient onto a bed to rest'],
 'The sequence is danger, response, help; the nurse shouts for help, activates the emergency team and checks breathing before starting CPR.'),
('What is the correct compression rate for adult CPR?',
 '100 to 120 per minute', ['60 to 80 per minute', '80 to 100 per minute', '140 to 160 per minute'],
 'Adult chest compressions are delivered at 100 to 120 per minute, about 5 to 6 cm deep, with full recoil.'),
('For adult CPR, what is the ratio of compressions to breaths with no advanced airway?',
 '30 to 2', ['15 to 2', '5 to 1', '50 to 5'],
 'With one or two rescuers and no advanced airway, adults get 30 compressions followed by 2 breaths.'),
('A defibrillator advises a shock for ventricular fibrillation. What must the nurse do before the shock?',
 'Ensure everyone is clear of him', ['Give a drug through the cannula first', 'Turn the oxygen off and walk away', 'Give two minutes of rescue breaths first'],
 'Everyone stands clear and oxygen is moved away from the chest before delivering the shock, then compressions resume at once.'),
('Which rhythm is not shockable?',
 'Asystole', ['Ventricular fibrillation', 'Pulseless ventricular tachycardia', 'Torsades with no pulse'],
 'Asystole and pulseless electrical activity are non-shockable; they are treated with CPR, adrenaline and treating reversible causes.'),
('A conscious adult is choking and cannot cough, speak or breathe. What is the first action?',
 'Up to five back blows', ['Give him a drink of water', 'Start chest compressions straight away', 'Perform a blind finger sweep of the mouth'],
 'For severe airway obstruction, five back blows are given, followed by up to five abdominal thrusts if unsuccessful.'),
('A patient with asthma has a silent chest and is exhausted. What does this indicate?',
 'Life-threatening asthma', ['Mild asthma, improving', 'Good air entry', 'An anxiety response only'],
 'A silent chest, exhaustion, cyanosis or confusion signal life-threatening asthma and need an emergency response.'),
('Which position helps a patient in respiratory distress?',
 'Sit upright, leaning forward', ['Lying flat on the back', 'Lying on the left side only', 'Head down and legs raised'],
 'Upright positions allow better lung expansion and use of accessory muscles.'),
('Which sign suggests a patient is developing hypoxia?',
 'Restlessness and confusion', ['Warm, pink skin and a calm mood', 'Slow, deep and restful sleep', 'A pulse rate that falls steadily'],
 'Early hypoxia causes anxiety, restlessness and confusion, followed by tachycardia, tachypnoea and cyanosis.'),
('A patient with COPD is on oxygen. Why is a controlled target saturation often used?',
 'A high oxygen level may reduce breathing drive', ['Oxygen damages the lungs in all COPD', 'Higher flows cost the hospital more', 'His saturations never change with oxygen'],
 'In patients at risk of hypercapnic failure, oxygen is titrated to a lower target range, such as 88 to 92 percent, per local protocol.'),
('Which of these is a typical sign of an acute stroke in the FAST test?',
 'Facial droop', ['Ankle swelling', 'Bounding pulse', 'Dry cough'],
 'FAST stands for face, arm, speech and time; a drooping face, arm weakness and slurred speech should prompt immediate emergency activation.'),
('A patient has crushing central chest pain radiating to the jaw. What should the nurse do first?',
 'Call for help, get a 12-lead ECG', ['Offer him food and let him rest', 'Give an antacid and observe him', 'Walk him slowly to the toilet'],
 'Possible acute coronary syndrome needs an urgent ECG, monitoring, medical review and treatment per protocol.'),
('What is the first-line measure for major external bleeding from a limb?',
 'Press firmly on the wound', ['Elevate the limb and do nothing else', 'Pull out any embedded objects', 'Wait for the doctor to arrive first'],
 'Firm direct pressure stops most bleeding. Objects embedded in the wound are left in place and padded.'),
('A patient with sepsis has a blood pressure of 80/40 and a raised lactate. Which action is a priority?',
 'Give IV fluids and antibiotics promptly', ['Encourage oral fluids and rest only', 'Wait for blood cultures to grow first', 'Lower the head of the bed and observe'],
 'Sepsis needs urgent cultures, IV antibiotics and fluid resuscitation, with escalation to senior review without delay.'),
('A patient is having a generalised seizure. What should the nurse do?',
 'Protect the head and note the time', ['Put an object between the teeth', 'Hold the limbs still', 'Give water after the seizure at once'],
 'Nothing is placed in the mouth and the patient is not restrained; the nurse protects the head, times the seizure and turns the patient on his side afterwards.'),
('Which finding in a patient with a head injury needs urgent escalation?',
 'A falling GCS with unequal pupils', ['A mild headache that is improving', 'Pink skin, a calm mood and a steady pulse', 'A pulse rate of 72 beats a minute'],
 'A drop in conscious level or unequal, dilating pupils suggest rising intracranial pressure and need immediate medical review.'),
('What is the first action for a patient in anaphylactic shock with stridor?',
 'Give IM adrenaline and call for help', ['Give an oral steroid and observe', 'Give IV fluids only and review later', 'Monitor and reassess in an hour'],
 'Adrenaline IM is given at once; airway support, oxygen and IV fluids follow.'),
('When using the ABCDE approach, what comes after Airway?',
 'Breathing', ['Circulation', 'Disability', 'Exposure'],
 'ABCDE runs airway, breathing, circulation, disability and exposure; problems are treated as they are found in that order.'),
('A patient on a ward collapses and the emergency team arrives. What does the nurse give them?',
 'A concise structured handover', ["The patient's full history first", 'No information until asked', 'A list of ward staff duties'],
 'A short SBAR or ABCDE-based summary of what happened, the observations and actions taken helps the team act quickly.'),
('A nurse is checking breathing and pulse in an unresponsive patient. How long may this check take before CPR starts?',
 'No more than 10 seconds', ['At least 30 seconds', 'About one full minute', 'Until the doctor arrives'],
 'Checks for breathing and a pulse should not delay CPR; if there are no signs of life within about 10 seconds, compressions begin.'),
]
